answer": "A", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "Correct statements: A, C and D. A — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “That was co-written by Jack Thorne, and “Lord of the Flies” can be seen as sort of a companion piece.” Giải thích: Phim được xem như tác phẩm “anh em” với bộ “Adolescence” của cùng biên kịch. C — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “This new “Lord Of The Flies” begins like the TV series “Lost,” which started with a close-up of a plane-crash survivor” Giải thích: Cảnh mở đầu gợi nhớ phim “Lost” — cũng về người sống sót sau tai nạn máy bay trên đảo. D — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “The soundtrack… relies greatly on angelic vocal arrangements because one group of young boys who survived the crash make up the school choir.” Giải thích: Nhạc phim dùng hợp xướng vì một nhóm bé trai sống sót chính là ca đoàn nhà trường. B, E, F, G — SAI Minh chứng: “in this case, it's the absence of social influences… as long as you don't call me what they used to call me… He's not a fatty. His real name is Piggy… I'm chapter chorister and head boy. I can sing high C sharp.” Giải thích: B sai (đảo chiều): trong “Adolescence” chính ẢNH HƯỞNG của mạng xã hội gây bạo lực; ở phim này mới là sự VẮNG MẶT của ảnh hưởng xã hội. E sai: Piggy xin ĐỪNG gọi biệt danh cũ. F sai: chính Ralph tiết lộ biệt danh. G sai: Jack nêu chức vụ trong ca đoàn và khả năng hát nốt cao, không phải thể thao."}, {"n": 14, "text": "Select one of the THREE true statements about the opening of the drama.", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "The critic regards this series as a companion piece to an earlier drama by the same writer.", "B": "In the earlier drama it was the absence of social influence that produced violence.", "C": "The opening shot recalls the beginning of another well-known series about an island.", "D": "The soundtrack draws on choral singing because some of the survivors belong to a choir.", "E": "Piggy asks the others to call him by the nickname he had at his old school.", "F": "Ralph refuses to reveal Piggy's nickname to the other boys on the beach.", "G": "Jack claims the leadership on the strength of his athletic ability."}, "answer": "C", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "Correct statements: A, C and D. A — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “That was co-written by Jack Thorne, and “Lord of the Flies” can be seen as sort of a companion piece.” Giải thích: Phim được xem như tác phẩm “anh em” với bộ “Adolescence” của cùng biên kịch. C — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “This new “Lord Of The Flies” begins like the TV series “Lost,” which started with a close-up of a plane-crash survivor” Giải thích: Cảnh mở đầu gợi nhớ phim “Lost” — cũng về người sống sót sau tai nạn máy bay trên đảo. D — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “The soundtrack… relies greatly on angelic vocal arrangements because one group of young boys who survived the crash make up the school choir.” Giải thích: Nhạc phim dùng hợp xướng vì một nhóm bé trai sống sót chính là ca đoàn nhà trường. B, E, F, G — SAI Minh chứng: “in this case, it's the absence of social influences… as long as you don't call me what they used to call me… He's not a fatty. His real name is Piggy… I'm chapter chorister and head boy. I can sing high C sharp.” Giải thích: B sai (đảo chiều): trong “Adolescence” chính ẢNH HƯỞNG của mạng xã hội gây bạo lực; ở phim này mới là sự VẮNG MẶT của ảnh hưởng xã hội. E sai: Piggy xin ĐỪNG gọi biệt danh cũ. F sai: chính Ralph tiết lộ biệt danh. G sai: Jack nêu chức vụ trong ca đoàn và khả năng hát nốt cao, không phải thể thao."}, {"n": 15, "text": "Select one of the THREE true statements about the opening of the drama.", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "The critic regards this series as a companion piece to an earlier drama by the same writer.", "B": "In the earlier drama it was the absence of social influence that produced violence.", "C": "The opening shot recalls the beginning of another well-known series about an island.", "D": "The soundtrack draws on choral singing because some of the survivors belong to a choir.", "E": "Piggy asks the others to call him by the nickname he had at his old school.", "F": "Ralph refuses to reveal Piggy's nickname to the other boys on the beach.", "G": "Jack claims the leadership on the strength of his athletic ability."}, "answer": "D", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "Correct statements: A, C and D. A — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “That was co-written by Jack Thorne, and “Lord of the Flies” can be seen as sort of a companion piece.” Giải thích: Phim được xem như tác phẩm “anh em” với bộ “Adolescence” của cùng biên kịch. C — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “This new “Lord Of The Flies” begins like the TV series “Lost,” which started with a close-up of a plane-crash survivor” Giải thích: Cảnh mở đầu gợi nhớ phim “Lost” — cũng về người sống sót sau tai nạn máy bay trên đảo. D — ĐÚNG (13–15) Minh chứng: “The soundtrack… relies greatly on angelic vocal arrangements because one group of young boys who survived the crash make up the school choir.” Giải thích: Nhạc phim dùng hợp xướng vì một nhóm bé trai sống sót chính là ca đoàn nhà trường. B, E, F, G — SAI Minh chứng: “in this case, it's the absence of social influences… as long as you don't call me what they used to call me… He's not a fatty. His real name is Piggy… I'm chapter chorister and head boy. I can sing high C sharp.” Giải thích: B sai (đảo chiều): trong “Adolescence” chính ẢNH HƯỞNG của mạng xã hội gây bạo lực; ở phim này mới là sự VẮNG MẶT của ảnh hưởng xã hội. E sai: Piggy xin ĐỪNG gọi biệt danh cũ. F sai: chính Ralph tiết lộ biệt danh. G sai: Jack nêu chức vụ trong ca đoàn và khả năng hát nốt cao, không phải thể thao."}, {"n": 16, "text": "What does the critic say distinguishes “Yellowjackets” from this story?", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "It was made for a British audience.", "B": "Its stranded survivors were teenage girls.", "C": "It had no adult characters in it at all.", "D": "It was filmed entirely inside a studio."}, "answer": "B", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "Câu 16 — B Minh chứng: “the variation being that the plane-crash survivors were teen girls, not young boys” Giải thích: Điểm khác của “Yellowjackets”: người sống sót là các thiếu nữ. Loại trừ: A, C, D: không có trong bài."}, {"n": 17, "text": "How do the two groups of boys differ as the story develops?", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "One builds boats while the other explores the island.", "B": "One keeps a fire alight while the other sleeps.", "C": "One provides shelter and food while the other hunts.", "D": "One returns to the plane while the other stays away."}, "answer": "C", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "Câu 17 — C Minh chứng: “one group is responsible and civilized, building shelters and gathering fruit and water, while the other hunts for wild game” Giải thích: Một nhóm dựng lán và kiếm thức ăn, nhóm kia đi săn thú. Loại trừ: A, B, D: không được nhắc tới."}, {"n": 18, "text": "The one significant departure from Golding's text concerns ______.", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "how long a doomed child survives", "B": "the identity of the elected chief", "C": "the location of the island itself", "D": "the eventual fate of the conch shell"}, "answer": "A", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "Câu 18 — A Minh chứng: “stunningly faithful to Golding's original text, except for allowing one ill-fated child to live a little longer than in the book” Giải thích: Khác biệt duy nhất đáng kể: một đứa trẻ xấu số được sống lâu hơn so với tiểu thuyết. Loại trừ: B, C, D: không hề được nêu là điểm khác biệt."}, {"n": 19, "text": "What is the effect of the way the wild-boar hunt is filmed?", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "It makes the violence seem unreal.", "B": "It keeps the audience at a safe distance.", "C": "It emphasises the beauty of the landscape.", "D": "It places the viewer among the boys."}, "answer": "D", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "Câu 19 — D Minh chứng: “filmed by Munden in a way that puts you right there with the boys” Giải thích: Cách quay khiến người xem như đứng ngay giữa đám trẻ. Loại trừ: B: trái ngược. A, C: không phải hiệu ứng được nêu."}, {"n": 20, "text": "The critic's overall verdict is that the series is ______.", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "more disturbing than the earlier drama", "B": "slightly easier to watch than the earlier drama", "C": "weaker than the two earlier film versions", "D": "too faithful to the novel to surprise anyone"}, "answer": "B", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "Câu 20 — B Minh chứng: ““Lord Of The Flies” is a bit easier to watch, but both of them are bold dramas” Giải thích: Nhà phê bình cho rằng phim DỄ XEM HƠN “Adolescence” một chút, dù cả hai đều mạnh mẽ. Loại trừ: A: ngược lại. C: ông không so sánh bất lợi với hai bản điện ảnh. D: ông khen sự trung thành với nguyên tác.  Your answers: Part 3. For questions 21 – 25, listen to part of a talk about the physics of crowds TWICE and answer each of the following questions with no more than TWO words taken from the recording. Write your answers in the corresponding spaces provided. 21. What single word describes the crowd the reporter and the physicist wade through?  22. What did the grains repeatedly do at the outlet of the silo before an obstacle was added?  23. What kind of forces does the physicist believe took over among the soldiers?  24. What term is used for the repeated circular movements traced by people in the plaza?  25. What is the physicist now studying that can ripple through a packed crowd?  PART 3 (21–25)"}, {"n": 21, "text": "What single word describes the crowd the reporter and the physicist wade through?", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "teeming", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "Câu 21 — TEEMING Minh chứng: “Zuriguel and I wade through a crowd teeming with folks thinking they're making their own decisions.” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Tính từ/phân từ chỉ đám đông “đặc kín, chen chúc”. Xuất hiện duy nhất một lần."}, {"n": 22, "text": "What did the grains repeatedly do at the outlet of the silo before an obstacle was added?", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "clogged", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "Câu 22 — CLOGGED Minh chứng: “Up to that point, the grains had always clogged the outlet every two or three seconds.” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Hạt liên tục LÀM NGHẼN cửa xả silo trước khi đặt vật cản."}, {"n": 23, "text": "What kind of forces does the physicist believe took over among the soldiers?", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "shear forces", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "Câu 23 — SHEAR FORCES Minh chứng: “that shear forces may have taken over, spinning them about and making a clean exit harder” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Lực TRƯỢT/CẮT được cho là đã chi phối khi 200 người lính chen đẩy. Chấm: phải đủ hai từ."}, {"n": 24, "text": "What term is used for the repeated circular movements traced by people in the plaza?", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "orbital motions", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "Câu 24 — ORBITAL MOTIONS Minh chứng: “These orbital motions consistently lasted 18 seconds” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường."}, {"n": 25, "text": "What is the physicist now studying that can ripple through a packed crowd?", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "pressure waves", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided. High on the fourth floor of a Boston hospital, reached by an unlikely route through the emergency entrance, stands a surgical theatre of 1821. Light pours in through the (26)________ above, and the room holds both a skeleton and an Egyptian mummy. A large painting dominates one wall. In it a dentist holds a glass (27)________ packed with an ether-soaked sponge, while a colleague removes a growth from a patient's neck. Ether is a colourless liquid that in small amounts may produce (28)________ or hallucinations, and in larger doses (29)________. Around the two men crowd students and physicians, (30)________ to see whether the experiment will succeed. It did. The operation of 16 October (31)________ is now taken as the birth date of anaesthesiology, although a Georgia doctor had used ether four years earlier without publishing his results. Until then surgery had been an ordeal: alcohol, (32)________ or a violent blow were the only alternatives. Historians suspect the theatre was placed at the top of the building in order to (33)________ the patients' cries. Today the room is described by one anaesthetist's father as a medical (34)________. His daughter, marking her first Ether Day, is glad of an occasion on which to (35)________ upon the responsibility her speciality carries."}, {"n": 26, "text": "Complete gap (26) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "cupola", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 26 — CUPOLA Minh chứng: “The louver windows in the cupola open to admit a flood of light.” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Phần MÁI VÒM có cửa chớp cho ánh sáng tràn vào phòng mổ."}, {"n": 27, "text": "Complete gap (27) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "sphere", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 27 — SPHERE Minh chứng: “He holds a glass sphere stuffed with a sponge soaked with ether” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Quả CẦU thuỷ tinh nhồi miếng bọt biển tẩm ether."}, {"n": 28, "text": "Complete gap (28) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "euphoria", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 28 — EUPHORIA Minh chứng: “a colorless liquid that can cause euphoria or hallucinations” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Trạng thái LÂNG LÂNG/khoái cảm do ether gây ra ở liều nhỏ. Ngữ cảnh đã cho sẵn “hallucinations”."}, {"n": 29, "text": "Complete gap (29) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "unconsciousness", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 29 — UNCONSCIOUSNESS Minh chứng: “or, in high enough doses, unconsciousness” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Ở liều đủ cao, ether gây BẤT TỈNH."}, {"n": 30, "text": "Complete gap (30) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "keen", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 30 — KEEN Minh chứng: “students and other physicians and surgeons, who are keen to know if this is going to work” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Tính từ chỉ sự HÁO HỨC muốn biết thí nghiệm có thành công không."}, {"n": 31, "text": "Complete gap (31) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "1846", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 31 — 1846 Minh chứng: “the day of that surgery — October 16, 1846 — that's considered the birth date of anesthesiology” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Năm diễn ra ca mổ lịch sử. ĐÂY LÀ CHỖ TRỐNG DẠNG SỐ DUY NHẤT; ngữ cảnh đã cho sẵn “16 October” nên chỉ điền năm."}, {"n": 32, "text": "Complete gap (32) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "distractions", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 32 — DISTRACTIONS Minh chứng: “Doctors might use alcohol, distractions or violently knock a patient out.” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Trước khi có thuốc mê, bác sĩ dùng rượu, cách ĐÁNH LẠC HƯỚNG hoặc đánh cho bệnh nhân ngất."}, {"n": 33, "text": "Complete gap (33) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "mask", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 33 — MASK Minh chứng: “the operating room may have been built on top of the hospital to mask the cries of patients” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Động từ chỉ việc CHE LẤP tiếng kêu la của bệnh nhân."}, {"n": 34, "text": "Complete gap (34) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "mecca", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 34 — MECCA Minh chứng: “Liao's dad, also an anesthesiologist, calls the Ether Dome a medical mecca” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Ẩn dụ “thánh địa” của ngành y."}, {"n": 35, "text": "Complete gap (35) in the summary: Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Thuật ngữ chỉ chuyển động vòng tròn lặp lại của mỗi người trong quảng trường. Câu 25 — PRESSURE WAVES Minh chứng: “Zuriguel's now exploring the pressure waves that can ripple through this crowd” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Sóng ÁP LỰC lan trong đám đông — hướng nghiên cứu hiện tại của ông.  Part 4. For questions 26 – 35, listen to part of a talk about the birth of anaesthesia TWICE, and complete the following summary with no more than ONE word and/or A number taken from the recording for each space. Write your answers in the corresponding numbered boxes provided.", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "reflect", "section": "Listening", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "Câu 35 — REFLECT Minh chứng: “I'm just really glad we have a day to reflect upon that.” Giải thích / lưu ý chấm: Động từ chỉ việc SUY NGẪM về trách nhiệm nghề nghiệp."}, {"n": 36, "text": "Ask teachers when they finally understood their subject, and most will name the year they first had to teach it. Psychologists call this the protégé effect, and a set of experiments at Washington University suggests that (36)______. Undergraduates read a passage about a military campaign; half were told that a test would follow, half that they would shortly teach the material to another student. In the event nobody taught anybody anything, and all of them sat the same test. Those expecting to teach recalled more of the passage than those expecting a test, and (37)______. They also answered more questions correctly about the main argument, though not about incidental detail. Since both groups read the same words for the same length of time, the difference must have lain in the manner of the reading, not its duration. The account favoured by the researchers is that the prospect of an audience recruits (38)______. Anticipating questions, a reader hunts for the thread of an argument instead of a heap of facts, pausing to check that it could be put in his own words. The effect has limits, however: (39)______, the advantage largely dissolves, presumably because nothing is any longer at stake. For students revising alone, the moral is agreeably cheap: (40)______. (Adapted from https://source.washu.edu/2014/07/expecting-to-teach-enhances-learning-recall/) 36. A. the mere expectation of having to explain something is altered by the way it is read B. something is altered in the way it is read whenever it has been explained to somebody\n\nChoose the best completion for gap (36).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "the mere expectation of having to explain something is altered by the way it is read", "B": "something is altered in the way it is read whenever it has been explained to somebody", "C": "the mere expectation of having to explain something alters the way it is read", "D": "the mere expectation of having to explain something altering the way it is read"}, "answer": "C", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 1", "explanation": "36. C — the mere expectation of having to explain something alters the way it is read. Sau “suggests that” phải là một MỆNH ĐỀ có động từ chia; chỉ C có động từ chính (“alters”). Nội dung cũng khớp trục lập luận của cả đoạn: chỉ riêng KỲ VỌNG phải giảng lại đã đủ làm đổi cách đọc — điều được chốt bằng câu “nobody taught anybody anything”. Loại A: đảo ngược quan hệ nhân – quả (kỳ vọng bị cách đọc làm thay đổi), trái hẳn thiết kế thí nghiệm, nơi kỳ vọng là biến độc lập được áp đặt TRƯỚC khi đọc. Loại B: “whenever it has been explained to somebody” đòi việc giảng phải THỰC SỰ diễn ra, trong khi đoạn khẳng định không ai giảng cho ai cả — bẫy logic nặng nhất của câu này. Loại D: “altering” là phân từ, không có động từ chia, nên không thể làm mệnh đề bổ ngữ cho “suggests that”."}, {"n": 37, "text": "Ask teachers when they finally understood their subject, and most will name the year they first had to teach it. Psychologists call this the protégé effect, and a set of experiments at Washington University suggests that (36)______. Undergraduates read a passage about a military campaign; half were told that a test would follow, half that they would shortly teach the material to another student. In the event nobody taught anybody anything, and all of them sat the same test. Those expecting to teach recalled more of the passage than those expecting a test, and (37)______. They also answered more questions correctly about the main argument, though not about incidental detail. Since both groups read the same words for the same length of time, the difference must have lain in the manner of the reading, not its duration. The account favoured by the researchers is that the prospect of an audience recruits (38)______. Anticipating questions, a reader hunts for the thread of an argument instead of a heap of facts, pausing to check that it could be put in his own words. The effect has limits, however: (39)______, the advantage largely dissolves, presumably because nothing is any longer at stake. For students revising alone, the moral is agreeably cheap: (40)______. (Adapted from https://source.washu.edu/2014/07/expecting-to-teach-enhances-learning-recall/) 36. A. the mere expectation of having to explain something is altered by the way it is read B. something is altered in the way it is read whenever it has been explained to somebody\n\nChoose the best completion for gap (37).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "what they recalled was organised more effectively around its central ideas", "B": "what they recalled were organised more effectively around its central ideas", "C": "what they recalled was organised more effectively around its incidental details", "D": "what was recalled by them organised more effectively around its central ideas"}, "answer": "A", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 1", "explanation": "37. A — what they recalled was organised more effectively around its central ideas. “What they recalled” là mệnh đề danh ngữ làm chủ ngữ, chia SỐ ÍT nên phải “was”; ý “nhớ có tổ chức hơn quanh các ý trung tâm” ăn khớp với câu ngay sau (“about the main argument, though not about incidental detail”). Loại B: “were” — sai hoà hợp chủ – vị, lỗi bị giấu sau một mệnh đề danh ngữ dài. Loại C: đúng ngữ pháp nhưng ngược logic — câu kế tiếp nói rõ lợi thế nằm ở LẬP LUẬN CHÍNH chứ không ở chi tiết vụn. Loại D: “what was recalled by them organised…” — “organised” ở dạng chủ động nhưng không có tân ngữ; muốn đúng phải là “was organised”."}, {"n": 38, "text": "Ask teachers when they finally understood their subject, and most will name the year they first had to teach it. Psychologists call this the protégé effect, and a set of experiments at Washington University suggests that (36)______. Undergraduates read a passage about a military campaign; half were told that a test would follow, half that they would shortly teach the material to another student. In the event nobody taught anybody anything, and all of them sat the same test. Those expecting to teach recalled more of the passage than those expecting a test, and (37)______. They also answered more questions correctly about the main argument, though not about incidental detail. Since both groups read the same words for the same length of time, the difference must have lain in the manner of the reading, not its duration. The account favoured by the researchers is that the prospect of an audience recruits (38)______. Anticipating questions, a reader hunts for the thread of an argument instead of a heap of facts, pausing to check that it could be put in his own words. The effect has limits, however: (39)______, the advantage largely dissolves, presumably because nothing is any longer at stake. For students revising alone, the moral is agreeably cheap: (40)______. (Adapted from https://source.washu.edu/2014/07/expecting-to-teach-enhances-learning-recall/) 36. A. the mere expectation of having to explain something is altered by the way it is read B. something is altered in the way it is read whenever it has been explained to somebody\n\nChoose the best completion for gap (38).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "the strategies which good learners are already possessed but seldom trouble to deploy", "B": "the strategies that good learners must first be taught before any teaching can occur", "C": "the strategies already possessed by good learners but seldom troubling to deploy", "D": "the strategies that good learners already possess but seldom trouble to deploy"}, "answer": "D", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 1", "explanation": "38. D — the strategies that good learners already possess but seldom trouble to deploy. Danh ngữ làm tân ngữ cho “recruits”, mệnh đề quan hệ chủ động đúng chuẩn; ý “người học giỏi vốn ĐÃ CÓ sẵn chiến lược, chỉ là ít khi chịu dùng” chính là kết luận của nhóm nghiên cứu và khớp với câu sau (khi có kỳ vọng, người đọc tự động làm những việc đó). Loại A: “are already possessed” — “possess” theo nghĩa sở hữu không dùng bị động kiểu này, mà vế sau lại quay về chủ động (“but seldom trouble to deploy”), gây lệch dạng. Loại B: mâu thuẫn logic — nếu phải ĐƯỢC DẠY trước thì thí nghiệm đã không thể tạo khác biệt chỉ bằng cách đổi kỳ vọng. Loại C: “seldom troubling to deploy” — phân từ hiện tại mang nghĩa chủ động gắn sai vào “the strategies” (chiến lược không “trouble”), phá vỡ đối xứng với “already possessed by”."}, {"n": 39, "text": "Ask teachers when they finally understood their subject, and most will name the year they first had to teach it. Psychologists call this the protégé effect, and a set of experiments at Washington University suggests that (36)______. Undergraduates read a passage about a military campaign; half were told that a test would follow, half that they would shortly teach the material to another student. In the event nobody taught anybody anything, and all of them sat the same test. Those expecting to teach recalled more of the passage than those expecting a test, and (37)______. They also answered more questions correctly about the main argument, though not about incidental detail. Since both groups read the same words for the same length of time, the difference must have lain in the manner of the reading, not its duration. The account favoured by the researchers is that the prospect of an audience recruits (38)______. Anticipating questions, a reader hunts for the thread of an argument instead of a heap of facts, pausing to check that it could be put in his own words. The effect has limits, however: (39)______, the advantage largely dissolves, presumably because nothing is any longer at stake. For students revising alone, the moral is agreeably cheap: (40)______. (Adapted from https://source.washu.edu/2014/07/expecting-to-teach-enhances-learning-recall/) 36. A. the mere expectation of having to explain something is altered by the way it is read B. something is altered in the way it is read whenever it has been explained to somebody\n\nChoose the best completion for gap (39).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "once learners will be told that they may keep the text in front of them while teaching", "B": "once learners are told that they may keep the text in front of them while teaching", "C": "unless learners are told that they may keep the text in front of them while teaching", "D": "once learners are told that they might have kept the text in front of them while teaching"}, "answer": "B", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 1", "explanation": "39. B — once learners are told that they may keep the text in front of them while teaching. Mệnh đề trạng ngữ chỉ thời gian dùng thì HIỆN TẠI để nói về tương lai/quy luật chung; nội dung cũng đúng: được phép mở sách khi giảng thì áp lực “bị phơi bày” biến mất, nên lợi thế tan đi — đúng như vế giải thích “nothing is any longer at stake”. Loại A: “once … will be told” — sau “once/when/as soon as” không dùng “will”. Loại C: “unless” đảo ngược điều kiện, thành “nếu KHÔNG được phép mở sách thì lợi thế mất” — trái hẳn vế giải thích ngay sau. Loại D: “might have kept” là thức giả định hoàn thành (việc đã không xảy ra trong quá khứ), không hợp với một quy luật đang có hiệu lực."}, {"n": 40, "text": "Ask teachers when they finally understood their subject, and most will name the year they first had to teach it. Psychologists call this the protégé effect, and a set of experiments at Washington University suggests that (36)______. Undergraduates read a passage about a military campaign; half were told that a test would follow, half that they would shortly teach the material to another student. In the event nobody taught anybody anything, and all of them sat the same test. Those expecting to teach recalled more of the passage than those expecting a test, and (37)______. They also answered more questions correctly about the main argument, though not about incidental detail. Since both groups read the same words for the same length of time, the difference must have lain in the manner of the reading, not its duration. The account favoured by the researchers is that the prospect of an audience recruits (38)______. Anticipating questions, a reader hunts for the thread of an argument instead of a heap of facts, pausing to check that it could be put in his own words. The effect has limits, however: (39)______, the advantage largely dissolves, presumably because nothing is any longer at stake. For students revising alone, the moral is agreeably cheap: (40)______. (Adapted from https://source.washu.edu/2014/07/expecting-to-teach-enhances-learning-recall/) 36. A. the mere expectation of having to explain something is altered by the way it is read B. something is altered in the way it is read whenever it has been explained to somebody\n\nChoose the best completion for gap (40).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "another pass through the chapter may do more for recall than rehearsing an explanation aloud", "B": "rehearsing an explanation aloud may do more for recall than to make another pass through the chapter", "C": "rehearsing an explanation aloud may do more for recall than another pass through the chapter", "D": "rehearsing an explanation aloud may do more for recall than another pass through the chapter would have done"}, "answer": "C", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 1", "explanation": "40. C — rehearsing an explanation aloud may do more for recall than another pass through the chapter. So sánh giữa hai DANH NGỮ song song (rehearsing… / another pass…), và chiều so sánh đúng với kết luận toàn bài. Loại A: đảo ngược chiều so sánh — đọc lại chương sách hoá ra lại hơn, mâu thuẫn với mọi kết quả vừa nêu. Loại B: “than to make another pass” — vỡ đối xứng giữa danh động từ và động từ nguyên thể sau “than”. Loại D: “may … than … would have done” — trộn tình thái hiện tại với giả định hoàn thành ở quá khứ, hai mốc thời gian không tương thích."}, {"n": 41, "text": "When Francis Galton went to a country fair in 1906, he was hoping to document the (41)______ of ordinary judgement. Some eight hundred visitors, few of them butchers or farmers, had each paid sixpence to guess the dressed weight of an ox led out for the purpose. Galton gathered up the tickets expecting a shambles; what he found instead was that the middlemost guess, 1,197 pounds, missed the true figure by less than one per cent. Scattered on either side, the individual errors had quietly cancelled one another out. The arithmetic works, however, only where the guesses err (42)______ of one another. Let a crowd deliberate, or let one confident voice announce a figure before the rest have committed themselves, and the remaining estimates (43)______ upon it; the diversity that made the average accurate is the first casualty of discussion. Stranger still is the discovery that a single mind can imitate a crowd. Asked to guess twice, with a decent interval between the attempts, and then to average the two, most people improve on either answer taken alone — a gain far smaller than the crowd's, yet by no means negligible. Expertise, paradoxically, (44)______ that advantage: when volunteers in one recent study were trained until their estimates scarcely varied, the second guess added nothing at all, though the outer crowd went on helping as before. For a generation accustomed to settling arguments with whoever replies first, the moral is not to trust the loudest voice in the room but to (45)______.\n\nChoose the best word or phrase for gap (41).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "frailty", "B": "fallibility", "C": "infirmity", "D": "vulnerability"}, "answer": "B", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "41. B — fallibility. “Fallibility” = tính dễ sai lầm, khả năng mắc lỗi của phán đoán — đúng với điều Galton định chứng minh (rằng đám đông ước lượng rất tệ) và là collocation chuẩn “the fallibility of judgement”. Loại A “frailty”: sự mong manh, yếu đuối về thể chất hoặc ý chí (“human frailty” = nhược điểm đạo đức) — không nói về độ chính xác. Loại C “infirmity”: ốm yếu, tật bệnh, nhất là do tuổi già — sai hẳn trường nghĩa. Loại D “vulnerability”: dễ bị tổn thương, dễ bị công kích, và đòi bổ ngữ “to something”; phán đoán “dễ bị tổn thương” không phải ý cần."}, {"n": 42, "text": "When Francis Galton went to a country fair in 1906, he was hoping to document the (41)______ of ordinary judgement. Some eight hundred visitors, few of them butchers or farmers, had each paid sixpence to guess the dressed weight of an ox led out for the purpose. Galton gathered up the tickets expecting a shambles; what he found instead was that the middlemost guess, 1,197 pounds, missed the true figure by less than one per cent. Scattered on either side, the individual errors had quietly cancelled one another out. The arithmetic works, however, only where the guesses err (42)______ of one another. Let a crowd deliberate, or let one confident voice announce a figure before the rest have committed themselves, and the remaining estimates (43)______ upon it; the diversity that made the average accurate is the first casualty of discussion. Stranger still is the discovery that a single mind can imitate a crowd. Asked to guess twice, with a decent interval between the attempts, and then to average the two, most people improve on either answer taken alone — a gain far smaller than the crowd's, yet by no means negligible. Expertise, paradoxically, (44)______ that advantage: when volunteers in one recent study were trained until their estimates scarcely varied, the second guess added nothing at all, though the outer crowd went on helping as before. For a generation accustomed to settling arguments with whoever replies first, the moral is not to trust the loudest voice in the room but to (45)______.\n\nChoose the best word or phrase for gap (42).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "autonomously", "B": "separately", "C": "individually", "D": "independently"}, "answer": "D", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "42. D — independently. Giới từ “of” đứng ngay sau chỗ trống khoá đáp án: chỉ “independently OF one another” là cụm chuẩn, nghĩa các sai số phải ĐỘC LẬP với nhau thì mới triệt tiêu lẫn nhau. Loại A “autonomously”: tự chủ, tự trị (of a region, a vehicle) — không dùng cho quan hệ độc lập thống kê và không đi với “of one another”. Loại B “separately”: đúng nghĩa “riêng rẽ” nhưng đi với giới từ “from”, không phải “of”. Loại C “individually”: “từng người một”, chỉ cách thức thực hiện chứ không diễn đạt tính độc lập, và cũng không đi với “of”."}, {"n": 43, "text": "When Francis Galton went to a country fair in 1906, he was hoping to document the (41)______ of ordinary judgement. Some eight hundred visitors, few of them butchers or farmers, had each paid sixpence to guess the dressed weight of an ox led out for the purpose. Galton gathered up the tickets expecting a shambles; what he found instead was that the middlemost guess, 1,197 pounds, missed the true figure by less than one per cent. Scattered on either side, the individual errors had quietly cancelled one another out. The arithmetic works, however, only where the guesses err (42)______ of one another. Let a crowd deliberate, or let one confident voice announce a figure before the rest have committed themselves, and the remaining estimates (43)______ upon it; the diversity that made the average accurate is the first casualty of discussion. Stranger still is the discovery that a single mind can imitate a crowd. Asked to guess twice, with a decent interval between the attempts, and then to average the two, most people improve on either answer taken alone — a gain far smaller than the crowd's, yet by no means negligible. Expertise, paradoxically, (44)______ that advantage: when volunteers in one recent study were trained until their estimates scarcely varied, the second guess added nothing at all, though the outer crowd went on helping as before. For a generation accustomed to settling arguments with whoever replies first, the moral is not to trust the loudest voice in the room but to (45)______.\n\nChoose the best word or phrase for gap (43).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "coalesce", "B": "gravitate", "C": "converge", "D": "conflate"}, "answer": "C", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "43. C — converge. “Converge on/upon a figure” = cùng dồn về một con số — đúng cả về nghĩa (mọi ước lượng bị kéo về phía con số được xướng lên trước) lẫn về giới từ “upon” đứng ngay sau. Loại A “coalesce”: hợp nhất thành một khối, đi với “into/around”, dùng cho các nhóm/khối vật chất chứ không cho các con số ước lượng. Loại B “gravitate”: bị hút về, luôn đi với “towards/to”, và mang sắc thái sở thích cá nhân. Loại D “conflate”: gộp nhầm hai thứ khác nhau làm một — là NGOẠI động từ, không thể đứng trước “upon it”."}, {"n": 44, "text": "When Francis Galton went to a country fair in 1906, he was hoping to document the (41)______ of ordinary judgement. Some eight hundred visitors, few of them butchers or farmers, had each paid sixpence to guess the dressed weight of an ox led out for the purpose. Galton gathered up the tickets expecting a shambles; what he found instead was that the middlemost guess, 1,197 pounds, missed the true figure by less than one per cent. Scattered on either side, the individual errors had quietly cancelled one another out. The arithmetic works, however, only where the guesses err (42)______ of one another. Let a crowd deliberate, or let one confident voice announce a figure before the rest have committed themselves, and the remaining estimates (43)______ upon it; the diversity that made the average accurate is the first casualty of discussion. Stranger still is the discovery that a single mind can imitate a crowd. Asked to guess twice, with a decent interval between the attempts, and then to average the two, most people improve on either answer taken alone — a gain far smaller than the crowd's, yet by no means negligible. Expertise, paradoxically, (44)______ that advantage: when volunteers in one recent study were trained until their estimates scarcely varied, the second guess added nothing at all, though the outer crowd went on helping as before. For a generation accustomed to settling arguments with whoever replies first, the moral is not to trust the loudest voice in the room but to (45)______.\n\nChoose the best word or phrase for gap (44).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "nullifies", "B": "forfeits", "C": "rescinds", "D": "revokes"}, "answer": "A", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "44. A — nullifies. “Nullify” = làm triệt tiêu, khiến một lợi thế trở nên vô hiệu — chủ ngữ có thể là một quá trình hay một phẩm chất trừu tượng (“Expertise nullifies that advantage”), đúng với cơ chế được giải thích ngay sau: chuyên môn làm co hẹp độ dao động nên chẳng còn gì để trung bình hoá. Loại B “forfeits”: mất đi cái gì như một cái giá phải trả, đòi chủ ngữ là NGƯỜI sở hữu lợi thế đó (“the expert forfeits…”), không dùng với chủ ngữ trừu tượng “expertise”. Loại C “rescinds” và D “revokes”: đều là hành vi CHÍNH THỨC huỷ bỏ một quyết định, luật lệ, giấy phép, lời mời — cần chủ thể có thẩm quyền, hoàn toàn lệch đăng ký với một hiện tượng nhận thức."}, {"n": 45, "text": "When Francis Galton went to a country fair in 1906, he was hoping to document the (41)______ of ordinary judgement. Some eight hundred visitors, few of them butchers or farmers, had each paid sixpence to guess the dressed weight of an ox led out for the purpose. Galton gathered up the tickets expecting a shambles; what he found instead was that the middlemost guess, 1,197 pounds, missed the true figure by less than one per cent. Scattered on either side, the individual errors had quietly cancelled one another out. The arithmetic works, however, only where the guesses err (42)______ of one another. Let a crowd deliberate, or let one confident voice announce a figure before the rest have committed themselves, and the remaining estimates (43)______ upon it; the diversity that made the average accurate is the first casualty of discussion. Stranger still is the discovery that a single mind can imitate a crowd. Asked to guess twice, with a decent interval between the attempts, and then to average the two, most people improve on either answer taken alone — a gain far smaller than the crowd's, yet by no means negligible. Expertise, paradoxically, (44)______ that advantage: when volunteers in one recent study were trained until their estimates scarcely varied, the second guess added nothing at all, though the outer crowd went on helping as before. For a generation accustomed to settling arguments with whoever replies first, the moral is not to trust the loudest voice in the room but to (45)______.\n\nChoose the best word or phrase for gap (45).", "typ": "choice", "options": {"A": "put your heads together", "B": "cast your net wide", "C": "hedge your bets", "D": "take a leaf out of their book"}, "answer": "B", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 2", "explanation": "45. B — cast your net wide. “Cast your net wide” = tìm kiếm trên diện rộng, thu thập từ nhiều nguồn khác nhau — khép đúng mạch lập luận: đừng tin một tiếng nói to nhất, hãy gom nhiều phán đoán hình thành độc lập. Loại A “put your heads together” = xúm lại bàn bạc cùng nhau — trái hẳn với phát hiện cốt lõi của bài, rằng thảo luận chung giết chết sự đa dạng làm nên độ chính xác. Loại C “hedge your bets” = phân tán rủi ro, không đặt hết vào một lựa chọn — nói về cách phòng thân, không phải cách thu thập ý kiến. Loại D “take a leaf out of somebody’s book” = học theo cách làm của ai đó — sai nghĩa và sai cả quy chiếu “their”."}, {"n": 46, "text": "Word bank: SOLICIT · PLEDGE · CHARITY · ARM · SQUANDER · POVERTY · BITTER · BEQUEATH · STINT · PLIGHT\n\nEvery December the appeals arrive, and with them an old question: does giving actually make the giver happier? In 2008 an experiment that has since become famous handed students a small sum of money and instructed half of them to spend it on themselves and half to spend it on somebody else. Those in the second group reported the better evening. The (0)______ impulse, it appeared, paid a quiet dividend to whoever obeyed it. Fifteen years later a replication with three times as many participants muddied the water. On the elaborate questionnaire used originally the two groups were indistinguishable; only on one blunt question — how happy did you feel immediately afterwards? — did the givers come out ahead. Warm glow, if it exists at all, is evidently a modest and fickle thing, and those who give (46)______, year after year and without counting the cost, are not thereby guaranteed a happier life. What the newer work does suggest is that the motive matters more than the sum. Money parted with out of unaffected (47)______ for somebody else's welfare lifts the mood; money extracted by embarrassment does not. Giving under duress, indeed, (48)______ the giver: the donation is made, but what is remembered afterwards is the resentment. Fundraisers grasped the point long ago. The face of one (49)______ family raises far more than any statistic about millions, for a face invites feeling whereas a number invites arithmetic. And the study that began it all retains an almost (50)______ simplicity: ten dollars, one afternoon, and a single question about the evening.\n\nWrite the correct form for gap (46).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "unstintingly", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "46. STINT → unstintingly. Nhóm 4 — phủ định chồng tầng, 3 tầng: un- + stint + -ing + -ly. Chỗ trống đứng ngay sau “those who give” và trước dấu phẩy, tức cần một TRẠNG TỪ chỉ cách thức; vế đồng vị “year after year and without counting the cost” khoá chặt nghĩa “cho đi hào phóng, không hề dè sẻn”. Bẫy: “stint” là động từ có thật (dè sẻn) nên học sinh dễ dừng ở “stintingly” — dạng không tồn tại trong từ điển; phải biết dạng phủ định “unstinting/unstintingly” mới đúng."}, {"n": 47, "text": "Word bank: SOLICIT · PLEDGE · CHARITY · ARM · SQUANDER · POVERTY · BITTER · BEQUEATH · STINT · PLIGHT\n\nEvery December the appeals arrive, and with them an old question: does giving actually make the giver happier? In 2008 an experiment that has since become famous handed students a small sum of money and instructed half of them to spend it on themselves and half to spend it on somebody else. Those in the second group reported the better evening. The (0)______ impulse, it appeared, paid a quiet dividend to whoever obeyed it. Fifteen years later a replication with three times as many participants muddied the water. On the elaborate questionnaire used originally the two groups were indistinguishable; only on one blunt question — how happy did you feel immediately afterwards? — did the givers come out ahead. Warm glow, if it exists at all, is evidently a modest and fickle thing, and those who give (46)______, year after year and without counting the cost, are not thereby guaranteed a happier life. What the newer work does suggest is that the motive matters more than the sum. Money parted with out of unaffected (47)______ for somebody else's welfare lifts the mood; money extracted by embarrassment does not. Giving under duress, indeed, (48)______ the giver: the donation is made, but what is remembered afterwards is the resentment. Fundraisers grasped the point long ago. The face of one (49)______ family raises far more than any statistic about millions, for a face invites feeling whereas a number invites arithmetic. And the study that began it all retains an almost (50)______ simplicity: ten dollars, one afternoon, and a single question about the evening.\n\nWrite the correct form for gap (47).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "solicitude", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "47. SOLICIT → solicitude. Nhóm 1 — hậu tố hiếm -itude kèm biến đổi gốc (solicit → solicitude). Sau “out of unaffected …” cần một DANH TỪ trừu tượng không đếm được chỉ TÌNH CẢM, và “solicitude for somebody’s welfare” là collocation cố định (sự quan tâm lo lắng cho người khác). Bẫy nặng: “solicitation” cũng là danh từ nhưng chỉ HÀNH VI mời chào/quyên xin (a solicitation for donations), là danh từ đếm được cần mạo từ, và không thể đi sau tính từ “unaffected” (= chân thành, không màu mè) vốn chỉ bổ nghĩa cho cảm xúc."}, {"n": 48, "text": "Word bank: SOLICIT · PLEDGE · CHARITY · ARM · SQUANDER · POVERTY · BITTER · BEQUEATH · STINT · PLIGHT\n\nEvery December the appeals arrive, and with them an old question: does giving actually make the giver happier? In 2008 an experiment that has since become famous handed students a small sum of money and instructed half of them to spend it on themselves and half to spend it on somebody else. Those in the second group reported the better evening. The (0)______ impulse, it appeared, paid a quiet dividend to whoever obeyed it. Fifteen years later a replication with three times as many participants muddied the water. On the elaborate questionnaire used originally the two groups were indistinguishable; only on one blunt question — how happy did you feel immediately afterwards? — did the givers come out ahead. Warm glow, if it exists at all, is evidently a modest and fickle thing, and those who give (46)______, year after year and without counting the cost, are not thereby guaranteed a happier life. What the newer work does suggest is that the motive matters more than the sum. Money parted with out of unaffected (47)______ for somebody else's welfare lifts the mood; money extracted by embarrassment does not. Giving under duress, indeed, (48)______ the giver: the donation is made, but what is remembered afterwards is the resentment. Fundraisers grasped the point long ago. The face of one (49)______ family raises far more than any statistic about millions, for a face invites feeling whereas a number invites arithmetic. And the study that began it all retains an almost (50)______ simplicity: ten dollars, one afternoon, and a single question about the evening.\n\nWrite the correct form for gap (48).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "embitters", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "48. BITTER → embitters. Nhóm 6 — động từ hoá bằng tiền tố em- (trước phụ âm môi b-): em + bitter. Đây là ô duy nhất cần một ĐỘNG TỪ CHIA THÌ; chủ ngữ “Giving under duress” là danh động từ số ít nên thêm -s. “Embitter somebody” = làm ai đó cay đắng, oán giận — khớp chính xác với vế sau “what is remembered afterwards is the resentment”. Bẫy: học sinh quen tay viết tính từ “bitter” hoặc danh từ “bitterness”, cả hai đều không lấp được vị trí động từ."}, {"n": 49, "text": "Word bank: SOLICIT · PLEDGE · CHARITY · ARM · SQUANDER · POVERTY · BITTER · BEQUEATH · STINT · PLIGHT\n\nEvery December the appeals arrive, and with them an old question: does giving actually make the giver happier? In 2008 an experiment that has since become famous handed students a small sum of money and instructed half of them to spend it on themselves and half to spend it on somebody else. Those in the second group reported the better evening. The (0)______ impulse, it appeared, paid a quiet dividend to whoever obeyed it. Fifteen years later a replication with three times as many participants muddied the water. On the elaborate questionnaire used originally the two groups were indistinguishable; only on one blunt question — how happy did you feel immediately afterwards? — did the givers come out ahead. Warm glow, if it exists at all, is evidently a modest and fickle thing, and those who give (46)______, year after year and without counting the cost, are not thereby guaranteed a happier life. What the newer work does suggest is that the motive matters more than the sum. Money parted with out of unaffected (47)______ for somebody else's welfare lifts the mood; money extracted by embarrassment does not. Giving under duress, indeed, (48)______ the giver: the donation is made, but what is remembered afterwards is the resentment. Fundraisers grasped the point long ago. The face of one (49)______ family raises far more than any statistic about millions, for a face invites feeling whereas a number invites arithmetic. And the study that began it all retains an almost (50)______ simplicity: ten dollars, one afternoon, and a single question about the evening.\n\nWrite the correct form for gap (49).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "poverty-stricken", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "49. POVERTY → poverty-stricken. Nhóm 2 — từ ghép đặc trưng chuyên Anh với đuôi -stricken (poverty-stricken, grief-stricken, panic-stricken). Cần một TÍNH TỪ đứng trước “family”, nghĩa “nghèo xơ xác”. Bẫy: bản thân POVERTY không có tính từ phái sinh trực tiếp nào khác (“impoverished” đến từ IMPOVERISH, không từ POVERTY), nên ai không biết mẫu ghép -stricken sẽ bí hoàn toàn."}, {"n": 50, "text": "Word bank: SOLICIT · PLEDGE · CHARITY · ARM · SQUANDER · POVERTY · BITTER · BEQUEATH · STINT · PLIGHT\n\nEvery December the appeals arrive, and with them an old question: does giving actually make the giver happier? In 2008 an experiment that has since become famous handed students a small sum of money and instructed half of them to spend it on themselves and half to spend it on somebody else. Those in the second group reported the better evening. The (0)______ impulse, it appeared, paid a quiet dividend to whoever obeyed it. Fifteen years later a replication with three times as many participants muddied the water. On the elaborate questionnaire used originally the two groups were indistinguishable; only on one blunt question — how happy did you feel immediately afterwards? — did the givers come out ahead. Warm glow, if it exists at all, is evidently a modest and fickle thing, and those who give (46)______, year after year and without counting the cost, are not thereby guaranteed a happier life. What the newer work does suggest is that the motive matters more than the sum. Money parted with out of unaffected (47)______ for somebody else's welfare lifts the mood; money extracted by embarrassment does not. Giving under duress, indeed, (48)______ the giver: the donation is made, but what is remembered afterwards is the resentment. Fundraisers grasped the point long ago. The face of one (49)______ family raises far more than any statistic about millions, for a face invites feeling whereas a number invites arithmetic. And the study that began it all retains an almost (50)______ simplicity: ten dollars, one afternoon, and a single question about the evening.\n\nWrite the correct form for gap (50).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "disarming", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 3", "explanation": "50. ARM → disarming. Nhóm 3 — bẫy gốc từ, 3 tầng: dis- + arm + -ing. Gốc “ARM” trông quen tới mức vô hại, nhưng dạng đích là một tính từ C2 đổi hẳn nghĩa: “disarming” = mộc mạc đến mức làm người ta hết cả đề phòng, thường đi với simplicity, candour, honesty, smile. Vị trí sau “an almost …” và trước danh từ “simplicity” đòi tính từ. Bẫy: “armed/unarmed/disarmed” đều sai nghĩa; “disarmingly” là trạng từ nên không lắp được trước danh từ."}, {"n": 51, "text": "Anyone who has ever stood at the very back of a supermarket queue (0) know the peculiar indignity of the position. Ryan Buell, who spent five hours watching shoppers at a single checkout, found that customers in last place were three and a half times more likely to change lanes than those with even one person behind them; of the seventy-one who switched, sixty-five did so from last place. Being last, it seems, deprives us from the one consolation a queue affords — the sight of somebody who is worse off. The consequences are not confined to hurt feelings. In a later experiment those left at the back abandoned the line more than three times as often as the others, and reported a level of dissatisfaction equivalent to seventy extra seconds of waiting. Removing the sensation altogether, Buell calculates, would bring a considerable difference to the system as a whole: in his simulations abandonment fell by more than two fifths and the number of customers served rose by an eighth. Service designers have accordingly begun to attach big importance to what the person waiting can actually see. Since the 1980s, when David Maister first set out his rules for the psychology of waiting, researchers study the gap between the time a queue takes and the time it appears to take. A queue which its end is invisible feels longer than one of identical length in plain view; occupied time passes more quickly than unoccupied time; and a delay that has been explained irritates far less than one that has not.\n\nWrite the correction for error (51).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "of", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "51. “deprives us from the one consolation a queue affords” → from → OF. Lỗi giới từ: cụm cố định là “deprive somebody OF something”. Bẫy ở chỗ “from” nghe rất xuôi tai vì tư duy “lấy đi KHỎI ai đó”, lại được giấu ngay sau cụm chèn “it seems”."}, {"n": 52, "text": "Anyone who has ever stood at the very back of a supermarket queue (0) know the peculiar indignity of the position. Ryan Buell, who spent five hours watching shoppers at a single checkout, found that customers in last place were three and a half times more likely to change lanes than those with even one person behind them; of the seventy-one who switched, sixty-five did so from last place. Being last, it seems, deprives us from the one consolation a queue affords — the sight of somebody who is worse off. The consequences are not confined to hurt feelings. In a later experiment those left at the back abandoned the line more than three times as often as the others, and reported a level of dissatisfaction equivalent to seventy extra seconds of waiting. Removing the sensation altogether, Buell calculates, would bring a considerable difference to the system as a whole: in his simulations abandonment fell by more than two fifths and the number of customers served rose by an eighth. Service designers have accordingly begun to attach big importance to what the person waiting can actually see. Since the 1980s, when David Maister first set out his rules for the psychology of waiting, researchers study the gap between the time a queue takes and the time it appears to take. A queue which its end is invisible feels longer than one of identical length in plain view; occupied time passes more quickly than unoccupied time; and a delay that has been explained irritates far less than one that has not.\n\nWrite the correction for error (52).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "make", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "52. “would bring a considerable difference to the system” → bring → MAKE. Lỗi collocation: tiếng Anh nói “MAKE a difference to something”, không bao giờ “bring a difference”. Câu vẫn đúng ngữ pháp và đúng nghĩa từ điển nên đọc lướt hoàn toàn không gợn — đúng kiểu bẫy kiểm tra vốn collocation."}, {"n": 53, "text": "Anyone who has ever stood at the very back of a supermarket queue (0) know the peculiar indignity of the position. Ryan Buell, who spent five hours watching shoppers at a single checkout, found that customers in last place were three and a half times more likely to change lanes than those with even one person behind them; of the seventy-one who switched, sixty-five did so from last place. Being last, it seems, deprives us from the one consolation a queue affords — the sight of somebody who is worse off. The consequences are not confined to hurt feelings. In a later experiment those left at the back abandoned the line more than three times as often as the others, and reported a level of dissatisfaction equivalent to seventy extra seconds of waiting. Removing the sensation altogether, Buell calculates, would bring a considerable difference to the system as a whole: in his simulations abandonment fell by more than two fifths and the number of customers served rose by an eighth. Service designers have accordingly begun to attach big importance to what the person waiting can actually see. Since the 1980s, when David Maister first set out his rules for the psychology of waiting, researchers study the gap between the time a queue takes and the time it appears to take. A queue which its end is invisible feels longer than one of identical length in plain view; occupied time passes more quickly than unoccupied time; and a delay that has been explained irritates far less than one that has not.\n\nWrite the correction for error (53).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "great", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "53. “begun to attach big importance to what the person waiting can see” → big → GREAT (hoặc considerable / paramount). Lỗi collocation: cụm chuẩn là “attach GREAT importance to something”. “Big” đi với difference, mistake, problem, decision — không đi với “importance” trong văn phong học thuật."}, {"n": 54, "text": "Anyone who has ever stood at the very back of a supermarket queue (0) know the peculiar indignity of the position. Ryan Buell, who spent five hours watching shoppers at a single checkout, found that customers in last place were three and a half times more likely to change lanes than those with even one person behind them; of the seventy-one who switched, sixty-five did so from last place. Being last, it seems, deprives us from the one consolation a queue affords — the sight of somebody who is worse off. The consequences are not confined to hurt feelings. In a later experiment those left at the back abandoned the line more than three times as often as the others, and reported a level of dissatisfaction equivalent to seventy extra seconds of waiting. Removing the sensation altogether, Buell calculates, would bring a considerable difference to the system as a whole: in his simulations abandonment fell by more than two fifths and the number of customers served rose by an eighth. Service designers have accordingly begun to attach big importance to what the person waiting can actually see. Since the 1980s, when David Maister first set out his rules for the psychology of waiting, researchers study the gap between the time a queue takes and the time it appears to take. A queue which its end is invisible feels longer than one of identical length in plain view; occupied time passes more quickly than unoccupied time; and a delay that has been explained irritates far less than one that has not.\n\nWrite the correction for error (54).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "have studied", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "54. “Since the 1980s … researchers study the gap” → study → HAVE STUDIED. Lỗi ngữ pháp: trạng ngữ “Since the 1980s” bắt buộc dùng thì hiện tại hoàn thành cho một hành động kéo dài từ quá khứ đến nay. Lỗi bị giấu sau mệnh đề quan hệ chèn giữa (“when David Maister first set out his rules…”) khiến trạng ngữ “Since the 1980s” cách động từ rất xa."}, {"n": 55, "text": "Anyone who has ever stood at the very back of a supermarket queue (0) know the peculiar indignity of the position. Ryan Buell, who spent five hours watching shoppers at a single checkout, found that customers in last place were three and a half times more likely to change lanes than those with even one person behind them; of the seventy-one who switched, sixty-five did so from last place. Being last, it seems, deprives us from the one consolation a queue affords — the sight of somebody who is worse off. The consequences are not confined to hurt feelings. In a later experiment those left at the back abandoned the line more than three times as often as the others, and reported a level of dissatisfaction equivalent to seventy extra seconds of waiting. Removing the sensation altogether, Buell calculates, would bring a considerable difference to the system as a whole: in his simulations abandonment fell by more than two fifths and the number of customers served rose by an eighth. Service designers have accordingly begun to attach big importance to what the person waiting can actually see. Since the 1980s, when David Maister first set out his rules for the psychology of waiting, researchers study the gap between the time a queue takes and the time it appears to take. A queue which its end is invisible feels longer than one of identical length in plain view; occupied time passes more quickly than unoccupied time; and a delay that has been explained irritates far less than one that has not.\n\nWrite the correction for error (55).", "typ": "text", "options": null, "answer": "whose", "section": "Language in Use", "part": "Part 4", "explanation": "55. “A queue which its end is invisible feels longer” → which → WHOSE. Lỗi ngữ pháp: quan hệ sở hữu giữa “queue” và “its end” đòi đại từ quan hệ “whose” (hoặc “the end of which”). Viết “which … its end” là kiểu đại từ lặp thừa (resumptive pronoun) — sai chuẩn tiếng Anh viết."}, {"n": 56, "text": "Few social rituals are observed with such fierce, unspoken discipline as the queue. Anyone who has ever attempted to slip in ahead of their turn will (56)__________ witness to the ferocity of the disapproval it provokes: a cough, a hardened stare and, in extremis, a public rebuke. Queues are, on the (57)__________ of it, monuments to fairness — first come, first served — yet the work of Richard Larson at MIT suggests that what rankles is rarely the wait itself but the suspicion that the wait is unjust. People will stand contentedly for twenty minutes provided nobody jumps (58)__________ of them; shave five minutes off that wait and let a single interloper through, and tempers flare. This explains why queue design is, first and (59)__________, an exercise in psychology rather than in logistics. Airports lengthen the walk to the baggage hall so that suitcases are already circling on arrival, thereby doing away (60)__________ the dead time that makes waiting feel interminable. Supermarkets install one serpentine line feeding several tills, which is fractionally slower on average but spares customers the agony of watching the neighbouring queue steal a (61)__________ on their own. Mirrors beside lifts, screens above check-in desks, piped music in call centres: all are calculated to take the customer’s mind (62)__________ the passing minutes. Contrary to national mythology, patience is by no (63)__________ a British invention. What differs from one culture to another is not the willingness to wait but the conventions that (64)__________ sway and the tolerance of physical proximity. A queue is less a line of people than a fragile contract — and one that everybody polices (65)__________ of charge.\n\nWrite ONE suitable word for gap (56