, when the French geologist Michel Siffre spent two months alone in a glacier cave with no watch, no daylight and no radio, telephoning the surface each time he woke, ate or went to sleep. His days stretched to a little over twenty-four hours, and by the time he came up he believed the date to be a month earlier than it was. Comparable experiments run in underground bunkers by the German physiologist Jürgen Aschoff produced the same result: stripped of every external cue, human beings do not become arrhythmic. They keep time, but slightly badly, drifting a few minutes later with each passing day. The internal clock is real, in other words, and it does not run at exactly twenty-four hours — which is precisely why it has to be reset every morning.\n\nB. The machinery that does the timekeeping turned out to be startlingly small. Working with fruit flies through the 1980s and 1990s, Jeffrey Hall, Michael Rosbash and Michael Young cloned the gene known as period and showed that the protein it encodes accumulates in the cell overnight and is degraded during the day. Once abundant, that protein blocks the very gene that produces it, so its own levels fall, the block is lifted, and manufacture starts again — a self-cancelling feedback loop that happens to take about a day to complete. Young later identified a second gene, timeless, whose protein escorts the first into the nucleus, and a third that tunes the speed of the whole oscillation. The three shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2017. In mammals the same logic runs in a cluster of some twenty thousand neurons sitting just above the crossing of the optic nerves, the suprachiasmatic nucleus, which behaves like a conductor. The conductor, however, is not the orchestra: nearly every tissue in the body — liver, pancreas, skin, even isolated cells grown in a dish — keeps a clock of its own, and these peripheral clocks go on oscillating for days after all connection with the brain has been severed. Entrainment to the world outside depends on a class of retinal cells characterised only in the early 2000s, which contain the pigment melanopsin and are exquisitely sensitive to blue wavelengths. They respond sluggishly, ignore fine detail, and send their signal not to the visual cortex but straight to the suprachiasmatic nucleus. People who are blind through damage to the rods and cones may nonetheless remain perfectly entrained, provided these cells survive.\n\nC. If every clock ran at the same phase, none of this would matter greatly. In practice the population is spread along a spectrum, from the small minority who wake spontaneously before six to the equally small minority who are barely coherent before noon. Chronotype is substantially heritable, shifts with age in a strikingly regular way, and lies largely beyond voluntary control. Children are early risers; the clock delays sharply at puberty, reaches its latest point at around twenty, and then advances steadily for the remainder of life. The German chronobiologist Till Roenneberg coined the term social jetlag for the gap between the sleep a person’s body would choose and the sleep their timetable permits, measured as the difference between the midpoint of sleep on working days and on free days. In industrialised populations the average gap exceeds an hour, and for a substantial minority it exceeds two — the equivalent of flying west across the Atlantic every Friday and back again every Monday. The finding has had one conspicuous practical consequence. Adolescents whose clocks have delayed by two hours are routinely asked to sit examinations at eight in the morning, and a number of school districts, most of them in the United States, have accordingly pushed their start times later. Where the change has been made and studied, attendance has improved and reported sleep has lengthened; the effect on grades has been more modest, and remains contested.\n\nD. The costs of living out of phase are easier to demonstrate in the laboratory than to quantify in the world. Volunteers kept on inverted schedules for a few weeks develop impaired glucose tolerance and raised blood pressure; mice given the same number of calories during their rest phase rather than their active phase put on more weight than mice fed at the proper hour. Epidemiological studies of night-shift workers report elevated rates of metabolic and cardiovascular disease, and in 2007 the International Agency for Research on Cancer classified shift work involving circadian disruption as a probable carcinogen — a classification resting, its authors were careful to state, on limited evidence in humans and sufficient evidence in animals. Whether the modern world has produced a sleep crisis is a separate question, and here the evidence is rather less obliging than the headlines suggest. Jerome Siegel and his colleagues fitted activity monitors to members of three pre-industrial societies — the Hadza of Tanzania, the San of Namibia and the Tsimane of Bolivia — none of whom have electric light, and found that they slept between five and a half and seven hours a night, no more than the average European or American, and that none of the three languages contained a word for insomnia. What the three groups did share was a consistent fall in temperature at sleep onset and a habit of waking before dawn. The moral Siegel draws is not that sleep does not matter, but that the industrialised eight-hour ideal may be a poor benchmark, and that the timing and the regularity of sleep deserve at least as much attention as its duration.", "The Cabinet and the Crowd": "The ancestor of the modern museum was not a public building but a private room. Across sixteenth- and seventeenth-century Europe, wealthy collectors assembled Wunderkammern, or cabinets of curiosity, in which a narwhal tusk sold as a unicorn’s horn might sit beside a Roman coin, a stuffed crocodile suspended from the ceiling, a clockwork automaton and a lump of ore. To modern eyes the arrangement looks like chaos. It was not. The cabinet obeyed a principle that has since fallen out of use: the deliberate juxtaposition of the natural and the man-made was meant to provoke wonder, and wonder was understood as the beginning of knowledge rather than as its opposite. [I] The Danish physician Ole Worm arranged his holdings so that a visitor moved from the mineral to the vegetable to the animal to the human artefact, tracing a chain of being rather than a taxonomy. Ferrante Imperato’s cabinet in Naples, engraved in 1599, shows shelves packed to the ceiling and a crocodile suspended overhead, an image reproduced so often since that it has become the emblem of the entire genre. Access was by invitation, and the invitation was itself a claim to standing; the collection said quite as much about its owner as about the world.\n\nThe transformation of the cabinet into the museum was legal before it was architectural. In 1677 Elias Ashmole presented to the University of Oxford a collection he had acquired — not without controversy — from the family of the gardener and traveller John Tradescant, on condition that a building be put up to house it and that the public be admitted. The Ashmolean opened in 1683. Seventy years later the physician Hans Sloane left his enormous holdings to the nation in return for a payment of twenty thousand pounds to his heirs, a sum Parliament raised by public lottery, and the British Museum opened its doors in 1759 to “all studious and curious Persons”. [II] In practice admission required a written application and a wait of several weeks. The decisive break came in 1793, when the revolutionary government in France opened the royal collections in the Louvre. What had been the property of a king became the property of a nation, and the museum acquired the function it has never since lost: the display of things held in common.\n\nThe nineteenth century gave the museum both its classifying zeal and its improving mission. Objects were taken out of cabinets of wonder and redistributed into series — by material, by period, by nation — so that the arrangement itself could be read as an argument. The change was not merely curatorial. Labels appeared on the cases, guidebooks were printed for sale at the door, and the visitor was for the first time expected to come away having learnt something rather than merely having marvelled. After the Great Exhibition of 1851 its financial surplus was used to found what became the Victoria and Albert Museum, whose first director, Henry Cole, opened the galleries in the evenings and lit them with gas so that working people might come after their shifts. [III] Cole was explicit that he was competing with the public house. The museum of this period was rarely neutral: national collections told national stories, and the ordering of the galleries frequently traced a line of progress terminating, conveniently, in the country that owned the building.\n\nIt is the acquisitiveness of precisely this period for which the museum is now asked to account. A substantial proportion of the objects in the great European collections arrived through conquest, punitive expedition or coercive purchase — most notoriously the sculptures removed from the Parthenon by Lord Elgin and the thousands of brass plaques and figures taken from Benin City in 1897. Defenders of the status quo replied in 2002 with a Declaration on the Importance and Value of Universal Museums, signed by eighteen institutions, which argued that objects acquired in earlier eras had become part of the heritage of the nations now housing them and that encyclopedic collections serve a global public. Critics observed that no museum outside Europe and North America had been invited to sign. Since 2018 the argument has moved: several European states have returned Benin material, and the question inside most institutions is no longer whether restitution may be contemplated but on what terms and at what pace. The legal position remains awkward. Several national collections are forbidden by statute from disposing of objects at all, so that even a willing board of trustees may require an Act of Parliament before it can hand anything back, and long-term loan has become the usual compromise.\n\nMeanwhile a quieter body of research has been steadily undermining assumptions about what visitors actually do. In 1916 Benjamin Ives Gilman, secretary of the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston, photographed visitors stooping and craning at badly placed cases and gave the resulting condition a name: museum fatigue. Later studies, using timing-and-tracking methods, found that visitors stop at a minority of the objects on display and, when they do stop, look for a median of under thirty seconds; that attention falls away steeply after the first half hour; and that most people turn right on entering a gallery. [IV] The findings have shaped practice rather more than they have shaped rhetoric. Interpretation panels have shortened, seating has multiplied, and the temporary blockbuster — ticketed, time-limited and heavily promoted — has become the financial engine of institutions whose permanent collections remain, in many countries, free.\n\nThat dependence is the source of the museum’s present unease. Ticketed exhibitions, cafés, shops and venue hire now fund a growing share of budgets, which makes attendance figures a measure of success and pushes programming towards the spectacular. Digitisation was expected to relieve the pressure by moving audiences online; in practice online visits appear to complement physical ones rather than to replace them. The museum therefore finds itself holding two incompatible ideas at once: that it is a place of slow, quiet attention to real objects, and that it must draw crowds in order to survive. Governments, meanwhile, have begun to attach conditions to their subsidies — on free access, on outreach, on the breadth of the audience reached — which pull the institution in a third direction again. Nothing in its history suggests that the tension will be resolved. The cabinet of curiosities was, after all, also a performance.\n\n(Adapted from Encyclopaedia Britannica: History of museums, https://www.britannica.com)", "The Grey Material": "After water, concrete is the substance that humanity consumes in the greatest quantity. Something in the region of fourteen billion cubic metres of it are poured every year, enough to lay a slab ten centimetres thick over every square metre of England. It is in the foundations of the building you are sitting in, in the pipes beneath the road outside, in the dam that regulates the river and the pier that holds back the sea. And precisely because it is everywhere, it has become invisible. Most people never notice concrete at all until the day it cracks.\n\n[101]\n\nThe Pantheon’s dome, poured in about 125 AD, remains the largest unreinforced concrete dome anywhere in the world; its builders lightened the mix as they rose towards the opening at the crown, swapping heavy basalt for porous volcanic pumice so that the structure grew lighter as it grew more precarious. For work in the sea the Romans used pozzolana, a volcanic ash from the Bay of Naples, and here they achieved something modern engineers still cannot match. Analysis of cores drilled from Roman harbour blocks has shown that seawater percolating slowly through the material grows a rare mineral, aluminous tobermorite, in the cracks. The harbours did not decay over two thousand years. They strengthened.\n\n[102]\n\nThe recovery began, as so often, with a practical emergency: lighthouses. Commissioned in 1756 to rebuild the Eddystone light on a reef swept by the Atlantic, the engineer John Smeaton could find no mortar that would set in a place that was under water twice a day. He tested limes systematically and discovered that the ones which hardened under water were made not from the purest limestone but from limestone contaminated with clay. In 1824 Joseph Aspdin, a bricklayer from Leeds, patented a fired mixture of limestone and clay which he called Portland cement, on the grounds that the set material resembled the prized building stone quarried on the Isle of Portland. The name has outlived every rival.\n\n[103]\n\nThe idea is generally credited to Joseph Monier, a Parisian gardener who in the 1860s wound iron mesh into his flowerpots to stop them shattering and then, recognising what he had, patented the principle for beams and pipes. The partnership between the two materials rests on a coincidence so convenient that it looks like design: steel and concrete expand at almost exactly the same rate when heated, so a reinforced beam warmed by the sun does not tear itself apart from within. There is a second gift. The cement paste is strongly alkaline, and that alkalinity forms a microscopically thin passive film on the surface of the steel which prevents it from rusting.\n\n[104]\n\nThe consequences are written across the building stock of the second half of the twentieth century. Car parks, bridge decks and balconies poured in the 1960s are spalling, their reinforcement swelling and their surfaces flaking away in sheets; engineers now speak of a wave of structures reaching the end of design lives which, in a great many cases, were never explicitly calculated in the first place. Repair is expensive, disruptive and frequently uneconomic. In several countries the annual cost of maintaining ageing concrete infrastructure has come to exceed the cost of building it in the first place, and the bill is rising faster than the budgets set aside for it.\n\n[105]\n\nManufacturing cement releases carbon dioxide twice over. Some comes from the fuel needed to heat a kiln to fourteen hundred degrees, which in principle could be replaced. The rest comes from the chemistry itself: heating limestone drives off carbon dioxide to leave the lime that makes the cement, and no change of fuel can prevent it. Cement production accounts for something close to eight per cent of global emissions — more than aviation and shipping combined. Substituting slag or fly ash for part of the clinker helps, but both are by-products of steelmaking and coal-fired power, industries that are themselves contracting. Novel binders, carbon capture at the kiln and the simple discipline of using less all have parts to play. The material that built the modern world will have to be rebuilt itself.\n\n(Adapted from “Concrete: the most destructive material on Earth”, https://www.theguardian.com)\n\nMissing paragraphs:\n\nMissing paragraphs:\nA. Cement on its own, however, could never have produced the twentieth century. Concrete is enormously strong in compression and pitifully weak in tension: press it and it holds, pull or bend it and it snaps. The breakthrough that turned a good paving material into the skeleton of the modern city was the discovery that thin rods of steel, buried inside the wet mix where the tensile stresses run, could supply exactly the quality the stone-like matrix lacked.\n\nB. Timber, by contrast, is enjoying an unexpected revival. Engineered panels of cross-laminated spruce are now used for apartment blocks of a dozen storeys and more; they arrive on site pre-cut, go up in a fraction of the time, and lock away carbon that the tree removed from the air while growing. Whether the world’s forests could supply construction at the scale cities require is, however, an open question.\n\nC. Invisibility of this kind is a modern luxury. For most of its history the material was a marvel, and the civilisation that first mastered it made no secret of the fact. Roman engineers called their mixture opus caementicium, and they used it to do things that dressed stone could not do at all: to vault, to span, and above all to set hard beneath the surface of the sea.\n\nD. Durability, however, is no longer the industry’s most uncomfortable problem. A structure that lasts sixty years instead of a hundred is a costly nuisance; a material that heats the planet is something else. The awkward arithmetic lies not in the gravel, nor the sand, nor the water, but in the grey powder that binds them together.\n\nE. Public affection for the material has never been strong. The Brutalist estates and civic centres of the post-war decades, admired by architects for their honesty about structure, were disliked almost from the moment they were finished, and the weather of northern Europe did the exposed surfaces no favours. Several have been demolished; a few have been listed; the argument between the two camps shows no sign of ending.\n\nF. Then the recipe was lost. For more than a thousand years after the collapse of the western empire, European builders reverted to lime mortars that would not set in water and were content to pile stone on stone. The knowledge did not so much decay as vanish, and what replaced it was inferior in every measurable respect. The Pantheon stood through the whole of that long interval as a reproach nobody knew how to answer.\n\nG. That protection, unfortunately, does not last for ever. Carbon dioxide from the air works its way slowly inwards and neutralises the alkalinity; chlorides from de-icing salt or sea spray attack the film directly. Once the steel begins to rust it swells, because rust occupies several times the volume of the metal it replaces, and the surrounding concrete is levered off from the inside."}; const KEY='emc_hw_68026'; let submitted=false;
